# text = Glegg considered , must be made to feel , when he came to his right mind , that he could never humble himself enough ; for _that_ had come which she had always foreseen would come of his insolence in time past Mr Glegg and Mr . Deane were less stern in their views , but they both of them thought Tulliver had done enough harm by his hot-tempered crotchets and ought to put them out of the question when a livelihood was offered him ; Wakem showed a right feeling about the matter ,-- _he_ had no grudge against Tulliver .
